skill: viral-reverse-engineering
version: 1.0.0
description: Scenario tests for viral-reverse-engineering. A run passes only when every item in 'expect' is satisfied. Written before the skill body (test-first).
evals[10]:
  - name, input, expect
  - happy-path-teardown, This founder's video about quitting his job got 4M views. Break down why it worked so I can learn from it., "reads brand-profile and audience first|deconstructs the piece layer by layer (hook, emotional/share driver, structure, packaging, distribution)|isolates the real driver from incidental features rather than crediting everything|runs a replicability check (account size, luck, one-time moment, sample size)|extracts a transferable principle and applies it to the user's niche|hands execution to the content skills rather than copying the original"
  - mechanism-not-surface-copy, Just recreate this viral video exactly with our product swapped in., "pushes back on copying the surface (same sound/topic/format) as the reason it worked|extracts the underlying mechanism and applies it with the brand's own angle/substance|notes that a surface copy is derivative, likely to flop, and risks ripping someone's IP"
  - isolate-real-driver, "It went viral because of the trending sound, right? Let's just use that sound.", "tests whether the sound was the actual driver or incidental (counterfactual: would it still have popped without it?)|identifies the more likely real lever (e.g., a relatable hook or emotional trigger)|warns that copying the wrong factor reproduces the noise, not the success"
  - survivorship-luck-honesty, A creator with 8M followers posted this and it blew up. What's the repeatable formula?, flags that a huge existing audience (and possible luck/timing) confounds the analysis|is honest that this may not be replicable for a small account and warns against a false principle|separates what's structurally transferable from what's account-size or one-time luck
  - why-people-shared, Why did people actually share this post?, "analyzes the share-trigger psychology (identity/self-expression, high-arousal emotion, social currency, practical value, relatability), not just describes the content|ties virality to shareability (people sending it to others), not just views|names the specific trigger most likely responsible"
  - sample-size-one-example, Here's one viral video. Tell me the pattern I should always follow., "notes that one example is an anecdote, not a pattern|recommends tearing down multiple viral pieces in the niche to find recurring mechanisms|frames any single-example takeaway as a hypothesis to test, not a law"
  - no-guarantee-viral, Use this to guarantee our next post goes viral., "does not promise virality; explains luck and distribution are partly outside anyone's control|frames the value as stacking the odds via proven mechanisms, not guaranteeing outcomes|stays honest rather than overselling"
  - bare-link-sourcing, https://www.tiktok.com/@someone/video/123456 — reverse engineer why this went viral., "explains it cannot watch the video from a link (platforms are walled)|runs the structured intake: asks for the hook, transcript/play-by-play, caption + on-screen text, stats, follower count, and top comments (or uses a subtitles/fetch tool if available)|does not fabricate frames, lines, or details it was not given|offers to proceed with whatever the user can provide and name the gaps"
  - partial-input-name-the-gaps, Here's the transcript of the video (no screenshots). Why did it work?, "analyzes the layers the transcript supports (hook, structure, angle, share-trigger)|explicitly names what it cannot assess without visuals (the visual hook, packaging, edit/pacing)|does not invent the on-screen/visual details it wasn't given"
  - no-analytics-visible-signals, Reverse-engineer our own top post from last month., "does not claim WoopSocial provides analytics/performance data|works from visible/native signals the user can see (views, likes, shares, comments) or pasted data|still applies mechanism analysis and flags small-sample caution on a single own-post"
